  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 1230 AM MDT Sun Apr 19 2026\n\nOn Monday, surface pressure pattern looks similar to Sunday, as\nlee trough continues along I-25 and tight gradient persists over\nthe sern corner. Kept the fire weather watch in place for\nBaca/Prowers counties as a result, as many models show the\npotential for gusty winds in the late afternoon/early evening.\nGenerally dry and warm with less wind elsewhere, though some\nmodest mid-leve moisture begins to spread northward across the\nmountains by late day, bringing a few sprinkles/flurries to\nmainly the higher terrain along the Continental Divide. Upper\nridge begins to drift east of Colorado Tuesday, with enough\nmid-level moisture for afternoon showers (maybe an isolated\nthunderstorm) over the higher terrain, though again surface\nlayer will be rather dry, so mainly sprinkles/flurries/wind with\nmost activity. Doesn't appear we'll have enough wind for\ncritical fire weather conditions at most locations, though will\nstill have to watch areas over the far southeast plains ahead \nof the lee trough, where surface gradient looks just strong \nenough to generate some gusty winds over 20 mph in the\nafternoon. Max temps continue their march upward, with 70s/80s\nwidespread at lower elevations, perhaps close to 90f over the\nlower Arkansas Valley. \n\nDeep upper trough moves into the Great Basin on Wednesday, with\nfirst piece of upper energy ejecting into the nrn plains on\nThursday. Set-up will bring strong southwest flow across much of\nColorado on Wednesday, with upper jet pushing into wrn Colorado\nduring the afternoon. Mid-level winds increase to 40-50 kts at\n7h, with 5h winds of 50-70 kts late in the day. As a result, Wed\nlooks hot/dry/windy, with high end Red Flag conditions over \nalmost the entire region as RH falls below 10 percent and \nsurface winds gust over 50 mph. There is even the potential for\nPDS Red Flag conditions in a few spots over the ern \nmountains/San Luis Valley/Fremont County, where winds will be\nstrongest. Still windy on Thu ahead of second piece of upper \nenergy sweeping through the central Rockies, though critical \nfire weather conditions are a little less clear cut, as moisture\nand perhaps some showers will reach the mountains during the \nday, while a cold front will sweep south through the plains at \nsome point as well. Still expect most of the plains to see \nhigh/extreme fire danger once again, though delineation of the \nactual area of critical conditions is far from clear at this\npoint. \n\nOnce cold front moves through late Thu, pattern shifts toward\ncooler/potentially wetter conditions as upper trough lingers\nover the Rockies and series of hard to time upper disturbances\nmove through the region. Model spread is rather high on timing \nand strength of various waves from Fri into the weekend, so\nstuck with NBM for now, which portrays a rather prolonged broad\nbrush of higher pops from Fri into Sunday.",
